1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,871
Section 9.1 ended with a real question left open: if nobody owns Monday's scoring

2
00:00:04,871 --> 00:00:07,718
step specifically, it's the first thing that slips.

3
00:00:07,718 --> 00:00:12,184
This lecture answers who "somebody" actually is, across the three ways this work

4
00:00:12,184 --> 00:00:13,300
genuinely gets done.

5
00:00:13,300 --> 00:00:16,258
None of the three is the correct one in the abstract.

6
00:00:16,258 --> 00:00:20,724
Sam runs Harlan's account solo, fifteen hours a week, and the sprint still runs.

7
00:00:20,724 --> 00:00:24,687
A larger brand might split it across a media buyer and a creative lead.

8
00:00:24,687 --> 00:00:29,208
An agency might run it for several accounts at once, each with a different client

9
00:00:29,208 --> 00:00:30,324
relationship on top.

10
00:00:30,324 --> 00:00:31,887
The five steps don't change.

11
00:00:31,887 --> 00:00:33,897
Who's accountable for each one does.

12
00:00:33,897 --> 00:00:37,134
What doesn't survive any of the three models is vagueness.

13
00:00:37,134 --> 00:00:39,200
"The team handles it" isn't an owner.

14
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:42,660
A specific name against Monday's scoring step, every week, is.

15
00:00:42,660 --> 00:00:44,893
Which model is actually yours right now?

16
00:00:44,893 --> 00:00:49,191
Not the one you're planning to grow into eventually, the one you're genuinely

17
00:00:49,191 --> 00:00:52,708
running this week, with the hours and people you actually have.

18
00:00:52,708 --> 00:00:57,285
This lecture is easiest to use if you answer that honestly before the table below,

19
00:00:57,285 --> 00:01:01,415
rather than reading it as three options to choose between in the abstract.

20
00:01:01,965 --> 00:01:05,670
Notice the solo row is the tightest constraint, not the loosest.

21
00:01:05,670 --> 00:01:10,416
One person doing all five steps means nothing gets caught by a second set of eyes,

22
00:01:10,416 --> 00:01:15,047
and it means the time-box from Section 9.1 matters more here than anywhere else.

23
00:01:15,047 --> 00:01:19,851
Without it, scoring quietly eats into the hours meant for generating, and the whole

24
00:01:19,851 --> 00:01:21,414
week slips a day at a time.

25
00:01:21,414 --> 00:01:26,045
The in-house row splits scoring from generating across two different skill sets,

26
00:01:26,045 --> 00:01:30,618
which is closer to how Harlan's setup could grow if the account scales further.

27
00:01:30,618 --> 00:01:35,307
That split only works if the two people actually talk on the same weekly cadence.

28
00:01:35,307 --> 00:01:40,169
A media buyer scoring in isolation, handing results to a creative lead who checks in

29
00:01:40,169 --> 00:01:43,758
irregularly, quietly breaks the order Section 9.1 insisted on.

30
00:01:43,758 --> 00:01:48,446
The agency row carries a responsibility the other two don't: reporting this whole

31
00:01:48,446 --> 00:01:51,572
rhythm to someone who isn't in the room for any of it.

32
00:01:51,572 --> 00:01:55,971
That's exactly what Section 9.5 covers next, and it's worth knowing now that

33
00:01:55,971 --> 00:02:00,544
whichever model you're running, someone eventually has to explain it to someone

34
00:02:00,544 --> 00:02:00,833
else.

35
00:02:01,383 --> 00:02:05,075
Three questions, and none of them has a universally right answer.

36
00:02:05,075 --> 00:02:09,846
The right model is whichever one actually gets all five of Section 9.1's steps done,

37
00:02:09,846 --> 00:02:14,220
honestly, every week, given the real hours and approvals you're working with.

38
00:02:14,220 --> 00:02:17,401
Notice question one is really about capacity, not skill.

39
00:02:17,401 --> 00:02:22,058
Sam could plausibly run a larger account than Harlan's solo, if the hours existed.

40
00:02:22,058 --> 00:02:26,772
They don't, which is exactly why fifteen hours a week and one account is the honest

41
00:02:26,772 --> 00:02:29,271
ceiling on what his current model can carry.

42
00:02:29,271 --> 00:02:33,702
Answer these for your own situation before the next lecture, which assumes you

43
00:02:33,702 --> 00:02:36,030
already know roughly which row you're in.

44
00:02:36,030 --> 00:02:40,745
The swipe file Section 9.4 builds works differently depending on whether one person

45
00:02:40,745 --> 00:02:41,881
feeds it or several.

46
00:02:41,881 --> 00:02:46,595
None of these three questions asks which model sounds more professional, and that's

47
00:02:46,595 --> 00:02:47,220
deliberate.

48
00:02:47,220 --> 00:02:51,877
A solo operator running Section 9.1's rhythm honestly, every week, is doing better

49
00:02:51,877 --> 00:02:56,648
work than a three- person team that's quietly skipping Monday's scoring step because

50
00:02:56,648 --> 00:02:58,977
nobody's individually accountable for it.

51
00:02:58,977 --> 00:03:02,555
The model is a means to the rhythm, not the achievement itself.

52
00:03:03,105 --> 00:03:07,452
One honest trap worth naming, because it looks different depending on which row

53
00:03:07,452 --> 00:03:10,918
you're in, and it's easy to assume it's someone else's problem.

54
00:03:10,918 --> 00:03:15,210
Working solo, the busiest weeks are exactly when scoring gets skipped, because

55
00:03:15,210 --> 00:03:17,796
there's no one else to notice it didn't happen.

56
00:03:17,796 --> 00:03:22,197
Working at an agency, the same skip happens for a different reason: a client who

57
00:03:22,197 --> 00:03:26,819
isn't reviewing the weekly numbers closely enough to notice a quiet week of unscored

58
00:03:26,819 --> 00:03:27,259
ad sets.

59
00:03:27,259 --> 00:03:30,175
Both failures look identical from the account's side.

60
00:03:30,175 --> 00:03:32,321
A winner nobody checked on for a month.

61
00:03:32,321 --> 00:03:36,943
Whichever model you're running, build in one honest check: something that would make

62
00:03:36,943 --> 00:03:41,234
a skipped week visible to you, not just something that trusts you'll remember.

63
00:03:41,234 --> 00:03:45,856
A shared calendar reminder, a client report due every Friday, anything that turns "I

64
00:03:45,856 --> 00:03:50,423
should score this" into "I have to show someone I scored this." Notice that the fix

65
00:03:50,423 --> 00:03:54,659
is the same shape across all three models, even though the failure mode looks

66
00:03:54,659 --> 00:03:55,210
different.

67
00:03:55,210 --> 00:03:59,831
Visibility, to someone other than the person doing the work, is what closes the gap.

68
00:03:59,831 --> 00:04:04,398
Working entirely alone removes that safeguard by definition, which is exactly why a

69
00:04:04,398 --> 00:04:09,020
solo operator has to manufacture it deliberately rather than assume a colleague will

70
00:04:09,020 --> 00:04:10,010
eventually notice.

71
00:04:10,560 --> 00:04:15,187
Next lecture builds the swipe file that feeds Wednesday's generation step, whichever

72
00:04:15,187 --> 00:04:16,289
model is running it.

73
00:04:16,289 --> 00:04:20,641
A solo operator and an agency pod both need somewhere to pull from that isn't a

74
00:04:20,641 --> 00:04:21,247
blank page.

75
00:04:21,247 --> 00:04:25,819
Before that, name the actual person, even if it's only you, against each of Section

76
00:04:25,819 --> 00:04:26,756
9.1's five steps.

77
00:04:26,756 --> 00:04:30,887
An unnamed owner is how a good rhythm quietly turns into an occasional one.

78
00:04:30,887 --> 00:04:35,239
Sam's setup is worth returning to here, because it's the least glamorous of the

79
00:04:35,239 --> 00:04:37,939
three models and the one that's actually working.

80
00:04:37,939 --> 00:04:42,566
Fifteen hours a week, one account, every step owned by the same person, with nothing

81
00:04:42,566 --> 00:04:46,863
more sophisticated than a calendar block protecting Monday's scoring step from

82
00:04:46,863 --> 00:04:49,507
getting eaten by whatever else that week brings.

83
00:04:49,507 --> 00:04:54,134
It scales further than it looks like it should, precisely because the rhythm itself,

84
00:04:54,134 --> 00:04:57,935
not the org chart around it, is what's actually carrying the account.
